ssh-otp — single-use SSH passphrases
===================================

Build on Debian with Zig 0.16.0 and libc development headers:

    mise exec -- zig build -Doptimize=ReleaseSafe
    mise exec -- zig build test -Doptimize=ReleaseSafe

Or invoke your installed Zig 0.16.0 directly. There are no third-party Zig
packages, package downloads, PAM development headers, or resident daemons.
Debian's libc development headers (libc6-dev), OpenSSH server and PAM runtime
are required. Python 3 is used for installation, not authentication.

Install (keep an existing trusted SSH-key session open):

    sudo python3 install.py install --user hayk

The build is deliberately separate: never run the Zig build as root.
The installer installs a root-owned setuid CLI and a root-owned PAM shared
library, replaces only sshd's standard common-auth include with the burner-only
module, validates effective SSH settings, and reloads ssh.service. It does not
restart SSH, create users, change passwords, change sudo policy, or remove
AllowUsers, AllowGroups, PermitRootLogin or other SSH account restrictions.

The supported starting configuration is Debian's standard PAM include structure
and key-only SSH authentication. Customized authentication stacks and existing
multi-factor policies are rejected rather than silently weakened. Configurations
with Match rules or external Includes require administrator review; the installer
checks effective settings for --user at localhost, not every possible SSH context.

Use --no-reload to stage and validate without reloading ssh.service:

    sudo python3 install.py install --user hayk --no-reload
    sudo systemctl reload ssh

Keep the trusted session open until a second session succeeds. On installation
errors, changed files are restored. SIGKILL or a machine failure during installation
may require manual recovery using /var/lib/ssh-otp-install/sshd.pam.original;
installation transactions are not guaranteed across power loss. Re-running an
installation with existing state is refused. Uninstall before reinstalling.

Usage from a trusted server session:

    ssh-otp hayk 10m

Type the displayed phrase at the SSH prompt on Windows:

    ssh hayk@server

If needed, explicitly choose the supported authentication method:

    ssh -o PreferredAuthentications=keyboard-interactive hayk@server

No download or SSH key is required on Windows. Obtain the server's host-key
fingerprint through your trusted session and verify it on first connection.

Only existing users are supported. Non-root issuers can target only their real
UID; root can target any existing account. Existing SSH account restrictions
still apply, so issuance does not guarantee that an account is allowed to log in.
The executable must reside on a filesystem that permits setuid execution.

Four uniformly random EFF words are joined by '='. Four EFF entries contain
hyphens, which are retained. Four words provide about 51.7 bits of entropy.
The phrase is printed once, only to a terminal, and is never stored in plaintext.
Your terminal scrollback may retain it. There is one outstanding phrase per UID.
A new issuance replaces the previous phrase. Five incorrect candidate phrases
invalidate it, so a remote attacker can cause denial of service to an issuance.

Duration is a positive integer with s, m, or h suffix, from 1s through 1h.
The deadline is measured in boot-clock seconds, including suspend; precision is
one second. Authentication consumes the phrase atomically, even if subsequent
account checks fail or the client disconnects. The established session is not
terminated by expiry. This is an SSH credential, NOT a sudo credential.

Ctrl-C, SIGTERM and SIGHUP revoke an outstanding phrase. A killed/crashed issuer
may leave the phrase active until its original deadline. PAM enforces that
original expiry independently. Stale expired records are removed on access or
reboot; the /run store is deliberately not persistent.

An untrusted client can steal the phrase before use or control your session.
It can also capture a reusable sudo password. This tool avoids leaving reusable
SSH keys behind; it does not make a compromised client trustworthy. Do not
forward an SSH agent to it. This is new privileged authentication code, not an
independently audited security product.

Uninstall:

    sudo python3 install.py uninstall

The installer restores the original SSH PAM file, removes its drop-in and
binaries, validates configuration, and reloads SSH. Uninstall refuses to overwrite
files changed since installation. --no-reload is also supported; existing daemon
processes can retain old configuration until reload. Outstanding runtime records
are removed. Existing sessions are not forcibly disconnected.

Verification
------------

The production CLI and PAM module are exercised using real Linux-PAM and OpenSSH
in an isolated user/mount/network namespace and chroot. No live server config is
changed. Run as your normal user, NOT host root:

    unshare --user --map-auto --map-root-user --mount --net \
        python3 tests/integration.py

Requires working subordinate UID/GID mappings and newuidmap/newgidmap (uidmap),
util-linux, Python 3, GCC, libc headers, iproute2, OpenSSL, and OpenSSH client/server.
The namespace test temporarily runs an SSH daemon on its private loopback only.
It uses the production binaries from zig-out, not a privilege-relaxed test build.
It verifies real-UID restrictions, unknown users, bounds, redirect rejection,
concurrent consumption, attempt limits, replacement, interruption, expiry even
after SIGKILL, real key and burner logins, continued sessions after expiry,
replay rejection, Unix-password rejection, uninstall restoration and rollback
when effective SSH configuration conflicts with the installation.

Wordlist attribution
--------------------

src/words.txt is EFF's large wordlist by Joseph Bonneau (July 2016), with the
five-digit dice labels removed and word order retained. EFF content is licensed
CC BY 4.0 unless otherwise noted. The wordlist is embedded at build time.

